<section id="section-6" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h3>FAQ</h3>
  </div>
  <div class={`${prose} space-y-6`}>
    <div id="faq-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">1. How do you get to Tokyo?</h4>
      <p>Tokyo is served by two main airports: Haneda Airport (HND) and Narita Airport (NRT). Haneda is closer to central Tokyo, while Narita is farther out but still well connected by trains, buses, and taxis. From Narita, the Narita Express is one of the easiest train options into central Tokyo, with direct connections to major stations such as Tokyo, Shinjuku, and Shinagawa.</p>
      <p>If you are already in Japan, you can also reach Tokyo by train. The Shinkansen, Japan’s bullet train, connects Tokyo Station with major cities such as Kyoto, Osaka, and Nagoya, making it easy to add Tokyo to a wider Japan itinerary.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">2. How do you get around Tokyo?</h4>
      <p>The easiest way to get around Tokyo is by train and metro. The network may look overwhelming at first, but once you start using it, it is usually the fastest and most convenient way to move between neighborhoods.</p>
      <p>During my trip, I used a Suica card, and it made getting around Tokyo much easier. Suica is a rechargeable IC card that you simply tap at the ticket gates, and the correct fare is deducted automatically. When the balance runs low, you can top it up and keep using it throughout your trip.</p>
      <p>Google Maps is very helpful in Tokyo, but always pay attention to the station exits. Large stations like Shinjuku, Shibuya, and Tokyo Station can feel confusing, and choosing the wrong exit can easily add extra walking time to your journey.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">3. How do you stay connected in Tokyo?</h4>
      <p>Having mobile data in Tokyo makes the trip much easier, especially for using Google Maps, checking train routes, translating menus, and accessing online tickets. I recommend setting up an eSIM before you arrive, so you’re not trying to figure it out at the airport after a long flight.</p>
      <p>For my trip, I used <a href="https://airalo.pxf.io/c/7435210/1268485/15608" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer sponsored" class={mapLink}>Airalo</a>, which was simple to install and worked well throughout Tokyo. The best thing to do is buy and install your eSIM while you still have Wi-Fi before your trip, then turn on the eSIM line and data roaming when you arrive in Japan. This way, your phone can connect once you land, and you can start using maps and train routes right away.</p>
      <p>👉 <a href="https://airalo.pxf.io/c/7435210/1268485/15608" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer sponsored" class={mapLink}>Get your Airalo eSIM here</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-3" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">4. When is the best time to visit Tokyo?</h4>
      <p>The best times to visit Tokyo are spring and fall, when the weather is usually more comfortable for walking and sightseeing. If you want to see cherry blossoms, aim for late March to early April, but keep in mind that the exact timing changes every year.</p>
      <p>For fall colors, Tokyo is usually at its best later in the season, around late November to early December. If you prefer fewer crowds and still want pleasant weather, late May or October can also be good options, although you should not expect peak cherry blossoms or fall foliage during those months.</p>
    </div>
    <div id="faq-4" class="scroll-mt-nav">
      <h4 class="mb-2 text-black">5. How many days do you need in Tokyo?</h4>
      <p>For a first visit, I would recommend at least 3 to 4 full days in Tokyo to see the main highlights without feeling completely rushed. This gives you enough time to explore a few key areas, visit some of the city’s most famous attractions, and get a first taste of Tokyo’s food, neighborhoods, and transport system.</p>
      <p>If you can stay 5 full days, even better. You’ll have more room to enjoy the city at a better pace, add a day trip, revisit an area you loved, or simply leave space for the unexpected moments that often end up being the most memorable.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-7" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class={prose}>
    <ArticleClosingNote label="Final thoughts">
      <p>Tokyo is not a city you can fully understand in one trip — and that is part of what makes it so exciting. In 5 days, you can see many of the city’s biggest highlights, experience completely different neighborhoods, and still leave with a long list of places you want to return for.</p>
      <p>Use this itinerary as a starting point, not a strict schedule. Some days may take longer than expected, some places may be busier than planned, and you may find yourself wanting to spend more time in one neighborhood instead of rushing to the next stop.</p>
      <p>The best way to enjoy Tokyo is to stay flexible, plan by area, and leave a little room for the unexpected moments — a quiet shrine, a great meal, a small side street, or a view that makes you stop for longer than you planned.</p>
    </ArticleClosingNote>
  </div>
</section>
